The same problem occurs between racks. Uplinks are the connections carrying traffic from a local group of servers toward the wider network. If those shared connections cannot carry all the servers' combined traffic at once, the network is oversubscribed.

A switch can therefore have plenty of internal capacity while the connections beyond it remain too narrow.

4. Work through the shared connection

Try this first: Eight servers each have a 400 Gb/s NIC. Each must send 2 GB out of its rack at the same time. Their shared uplinks can carry 1.6 Tb/s in that direction. If capacity is shared equally, what is the shortest transfer time?

These are illustrative assumptions. Ignore other traffic and protocol overhead—the extra information sent alongside the useful data.

First, keep bits and bytes separate. Lowercase b means bits; uppercase B means bytes. There are eight bits in one byte.

One NIC can send:

400 Gb/s ÷ 8 = 50 GB/s.

If the NIC were the only limit:

2 GB ÷ 50 GB/s = 0.04 seconds = 40 milliseconds.

But all eight servers together request:

8 × 400 Gb/s = 3.2 Tb/s.

The shared uplinks provide only 1.6 Tb/s, half that amount. Each server therefore gets 200 Gb/s, or 25 GB/s.

The actual lower bound is:

2 GB ÷ 25 GB/s = 0.08 seconds = 80 milliseconds.

The tempting answer is 40 milliseconds. That uses the NIC speed but ignores the connection all eight servers share. Real transfers may take longer once additional overhead is included.

5. Find the bottleneck before buying faster hardware

Measure how much data crosses each part of the path.

If data leaves the GPU slowly because the PCIe path is limiting, a faster network switch cannot fix that.

If the NIC port is fully busy while the shared uplinks have spare capacity, the NIC may be the limit. If the uplinks are full while individual NICs run below their maximum, the shared connection may be the limit.

If some paths have long queues while others are quiet, traffic may be distributed unevenly.

Then test one change: add uplink capacity, send fewer bytes or place frequently communicating GPUs closer together. Check whether the data exchange and the whole training step actually get shorter. If neither improves, another constraint may be responsible.

6. What this means for the supply chain

A usable path needs GPU connections, NICs, switch chips, boards, connectors, cables and software that chooses how to use them.

Doubling NIC speed may also require more PCIe capacity, faster switch ports and more uplinks. One faster component can otherwise leave the overall job almost unchanged.

For example, NVIDIA documents ConnectX-7 cards supporting 400 Gb/s networking and PCIe x16 connectivity. The x16 describes 16 PCIe lanes, or parallel signaling paths. Those specifications describe hardware capability; they do not guarantee the application will sustain that speed.

When assessing demand, ask which complete paths need upgrading and whether communication is actually slowing the workload.

Remember

- The NIC moves data between a server's memory and the network.
- The switch directs packets; shared outgoing connections can make them wait.
- Transfer speed depends on the narrowest usable part of the whole path.

